-- title:
--   Frobenius's density theorem, qualitative form
-- statement:
--   Let $L$ be a field that is a number field and Galois over $\mathbb{Q}$. The assertion [`FrobeniusDensity.Statement L`](def/TaylorWiles_Primes.html#L72) holds: for every automorphism $\sigma \in \operatorname{Gal}(L/\mathbb{Q})$ (the Lean type `L ≃ₐ[ℚ] L`) and every finite set $S$ of natural numbers, there is a natural number $\ell \notin S$ satisfying `RealizesCyclicAt L σ ℓ`, that is: $\ell$ is prime, and for every prime ideal $Q$ of $\mathcal{O}_L$ which lies over the ideal of $\mathbb{Z}$ generated by $\ell$ and whose residue ring $\mathcal{O}_L/Q$ is finite, there exists a natural number $k$ coprime to the order of $\sigma$ such that $\sigma^k$ is conjugate in $\operatorname{Gal}(L/\mathbb{Q})$ to the arithmetic Frobenius `arithFrobAt ℤ (L ≃ₐ[ℚ] L) Q` at $Q$. Thus the conclusion produces, outside any prescribed finite exceptional set of rational primes, a prime $\ell$ whose Frobenius conjugacy classes at all primes above it are classes of generators of the cyclic group $\langle\sigma\rangle$; no statement is made about which power $k$ occurs, nor is $S$ required to consist of primes.
--
--   This is the qualitative form of Frobenius's density theorem, the predecessor of Chebotarev's theorem: every cyclic subgroup of $\operatorname{Gal}(L/\mathbb{Q})$ is realised as a decomposition subgroup at infinitely many rational primes. In the present development it is the source of auxiliary primes with prescribed Frobenius behaviour, used in the construction of Taylor–Wiles primes, in the choice of auxiliary primes for level lowering, and in the identification of two-dimensional Galois representations by traces.
